WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:02.334
-Our next guest
is a talented actress

00:00:02.334 --> 00:00:04.000
you know from movies
like "Scream 3,"

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:05.467
"Party Girl,"
and "Best in Show."

00:00:05.467 --> 00:00:08.200
She stars in "Beau Is Afraid,"
which is in theaters now.

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:09.200
Let's take a look.

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:11.601
-I can't believe it's you.

00:00:12.601 --> 00:00:14.601
[Music plays over radio]

00:00:14.601 --> 00:00:16.801
-I can't believe it's you.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:21.601
-Do I look different?

00:00:21.601 --> 00:00:23.601
You recognize me?

00:00:23.601 --> 00:00:24.801
I'm old now.

00:00:26.467 --> 00:00:27.534
-You're the same.

00:00:29.934 --> 00:00:31.100
-No, I'm not.

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:33.100
-You are.

00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:35.601
-Well, you're the same, too.

00:00:35.601 --> 00:00:37.734
Except for your body
and your face.

00:00:39.701 --> 00:00:40.968
-Please welcome to the show.

00:00:40.968 --> 00:00:42.634
Parker Posey, everyone.

00:00:42.634 --> 00:00:52.334
♪♪

00:00:52.334 --> 00:01:02.100
♪♪

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:03.634
Hi!
-Hi!

00:01:03.634 --> 00:01:05.968
-Fancy white boots?
Don't mind if I do.

00:01:05.968 --> 00:01:07.167
-Thank you!
This is...

00:01:07.167 --> 00:01:09.501
I'm giving you Agnes Moorehead
from "Bewitched."

00:01:09.501 --> 00:01:10.968
-[ Chuckles ]
Oh, thank you for that.

00:01:10.968 --> 00:01:12.100
-Yeah, you're welcome.
-Thank you very much.

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:13.467
It's so lovely
to have you here.

00:01:13.467 --> 00:01:15.934
-I know. I can't believe
I've never been on your show.

00:01:15.934 --> 00:01:17.234
-Because we've been
lucky enough to run

00:01:17.234 --> 00:01:18.334
into each over the years.

00:01:18.334 --> 00:01:19.868
-That's right.
-You were --

00:01:19.868 --> 00:01:22.634
So we were
at a wedding together.

00:01:22.634 --> 00:01:24.934
Our friend Chris Kattan.
-Chris Kattan at Yosemite.

00:01:24.934 --> 00:01:26.100
-It was in Yosemite.

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:29.234
-Forest fires like big fires,
very smokey.

00:01:29.234 --> 00:01:31.567
-Yeah, choked -- It was the
Smokiest wedding I've been at.

00:01:31.567 --> 00:01:33.167
-Yes. Yes, [Laughing]
it was very smokey.

00:01:33.167 --> 00:01:34.501
-It was very smokey.
But that's where I met...

00:01:34.501 --> 00:01:35.801
-I think it was staining
people's,

00:01:35.801 --> 00:01:36.801
you know,
clothes and stuff.

00:01:36.801 --> 00:01:38.300
-Yes. When it was over,

00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:39.601
we all looked like
chimney sweeps.

00:01:39.601 --> 00:01:41.133
-[Chuckles]
That's right.

00:01:41.133 --> 00:01:42.501
-You were the best man
at that wedding.

00:01:42.501 --> 00:01:44.300
-That's right.
-You spoke on behalf of Chris.

00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:45.467
-That's right.

00:01:45.467 --> 00:01:47.033
-And how -- Well, did you enjoy
that experience?

00:01:47.033 --> 00:01:48.200
-I did.

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:49.200
-You did a wonderful speech.
-I did.

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:50.434
-We had a -- Thank you.

00:01:50.434 --> 00:01:52.300
And it was in front
of a lot of comedians.

00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:53.601
-There were a lot of comedians.
Yeah. That raises the stakes.

00:01:53.601 --> 00:01:55.334
-Jennifer Coolidge.
-Jennifer Coolidge was there.

00:01:55.334 --> 00:01:57.267
-Brooke Shields was there, too.
-Brook Shields was there.

00:01:57.267 --> 00:01:58.901
It was a real A-list affair.
Yeah.

00:01:58.901 --> 00:01:59.901
[Audience laughs]

00:01:59.901 --> 00:02:02.100
-Have you seen her, doc?

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:03.634
- I have seen "Pretty Baby."
-On Hulu?

00:02:03.634 --> 00:02:05.534
-The Brook -- It was wonderful.
-Oh, my God. It was so good.

00:02:05.534 --> 00:02:06.868
-It was great.
-I watched it over the weekend.

00:02:06.868 --> 00:02:08.667
-Here we go, great. Look at us.
-Yeah. Yeah, I know.

00:02:08.667 --> 00:02:10.334
[Audience laughs]

00:02:10.334 --> 00:02:12.534
-You've been a New Yorker
since the early '90s, correct?

00:02:12.534 --> 00:02:13.601
-That's right. Yeah.

00:02:13.601 --> 00:02:15.067
-How do you feel
like New York is doing?

00:02:15.067 --> 00:02:16.434
Because you've stayed --
you've stayed the course?

00:02:16.434 --> 00:02:17.667
-I know. You know,

00:02:17.667 --> 00:02:20.734
I haven't lived in the city
in like six years.

00:02:20.734 --> 00:02:24.267
I've been back and forth,
and I'm a '90s girl.

00:02:24.267 --> 00:02:26.601
I'm really happy the '90s
are back now.

00:02:26.601 --> 00:02:27.601
-Okay.

00:02:27.601 --> 00:02:29.834
People seem to be,
like, having fun.

00:02:29.834 --> 00:02:31.701
-Yeah.
-And, like, I was in Atlanta.

00:02:31.701 --> 00:02:32.801
I was working in Atlanta.

00:02:32.801 --> 00:02:34.601
and I had a Lyft driver
and I called her.

00:02:34.601 --> 00:02:36.868
And she had her hair
all curled up.

00:02:36.868 --> 00:02:38.634
She was going out that night.

00:02:38.634 --> 00:02:41.033
Hair in curlers,
did not care

00:02:41.033 --> 00:02:43.133
because she was going out
to have a good time later.

00:02:43.133 --> 00:02:44.501
-Great.
-I love that.

00:02:44.501 --> 00:02:47.400
-I think I worked with
some young people on the play

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:49.133
that I just did,
and they're...

00:02:49.133 --> 00:02:50.267
-And young people
are happy to be here?

00:02:50.267 --> 00:02:51.634
-They're happy to be here.
-That's great.

00:02:51.634 --> 00:02:55.601
-And they like the vibe.
And I'm just -- I'm --

00:02:55.601 --> 00:02:57.467
You know, it's the people
that make New York.

00:02:57.467 --> 00:02:59.501
It's like walking
on the sidewalks.

00:02:59.501 --> 00:03:02.701
It's saying hi to the dogs.
It's looking in storefronts.

00:03:02.701 --> 00:03:05.367
It's connecting to everybody
and writing with that energy

00:03:05.367 --> 00:03:06.934
and being spontaneous.

00:03:06.934 --> 00:03:08.267
And that's why you live
in New York.

00:03:08.267 --> 00:03:09.467
-There you go.

00:03:09.467 --> 00:03:12.801
-I can complain about
how expensive wine is.

00:03:12.801 --> 00:03:14.801
Like a glass of wine
is like $20.

00:03:14.801 --> 00:03:16.701
-Right. By the glass.
-I think that is wrong.

00:03:16.701 --> 00:03:18.000
-Yes.
-I mean, remember...

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:20.567
- There should be -- Adam's
should put a wine cap

00:03:20.567 --> 00:03:22.367
on how much
you can charge per glass.

00:03:22.367 --> 00:03:24.033
-It's like that much, too.
-Yeah.

00:03:24.033 --> 00:03:25.434
-It's that's --
That's not...

00:03:25.434 --> 00:03:26.868
-And then when you bring
your own bottle of wine

00:03:26.868 --> 00:03:28.300
to a restaurant
and pour it under the table,

00:03:28.300 --> 00:03:29.300
they give you a hard time.

00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:30.634
-That's right.

00:03:30.634 --> 00:03:32.367
They sure do.
-[Chuckles]

00:03:32.367 --> 00:03:36.501
Hey, so this is -- this
is fantastically a weird film,

00:03:36.501 --> 00:03:37.734
a wonderfully weird film.

00:03:37.734 --> 00:03:39.667
Joaquin Phoenix
and the director, Ari Aster,

00:03:39.667 --> 00:03:41.567
has made some of the...
-He's incredible.

00:03:41.567 --> 00:03:43.601
-He really -- He made
"Hereditary," "Midsommar."

00:03:43.601 --> 00:03:45.868
These are some of the...
-Is everyone an Ari Aster fan?

00:03:45.868 --> 00:03:48.167
[Cheers and applause]

00:03:48.167 --> 00:03:52.734
I mean, we don't support a lot
of auteurs in this country.

00:03:52.734 --> 00:03:55.467
And by that I mean
directors and writers

00:03:55.467 --> 00:03:57.801
making their own film
and having their own vision.

00:03:57.801 --> 00:03:59.734
Quentin Tarantino,
for example.

00:03:59.734 --> 00:04:03.634
So I think Ari is a voice
of his generation,

00:04:03.634 --> 00:04:05.801
a real visionary.

00:04:05.801 --> 00:04:08.634
-And I was just so excited
to be a part of this.

00:04:08.634 --> 00:04:10.000
-It's really fun to sit down
and watch one of his movies,

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:12.968
and know that it's not going
to go where you think,

00:04:12.968 --> 00:04:14.434
but you are very confident

00:04:14.434 --> 00:04:17.367
that the person who made it
knows exactly why it's -- Yeah.

00:04:17.367 --> 00:04:19.434
-He'll elicit
a certain feeling like,

00:04:19.434 --> 00:04:20.701
So that scene you just saw,

00:04:20.701 --> 00:04:22.634
Joaquin and I were laughing
our heads off.

00:04:22.634 --> 00:04:24.400
We just thought
it was so funny.

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:27.267
You know, this guy
is so damaged to the like,

00:04:27.267 --> 00:04:29.534
he might as well be in an opera.

00:04:29.534 --> 00:04:31.901
When I saw him
for the first time,

00:04:31.901 --> 00:04:35.167
like, I thought
he was like a worker on set.

00:04:35.167 --> 00:04:37.601
He came in, he was dressed
in these khaki things

00:04:37.601 --> 00:04:39.100
and his head was on him.

00:04:39.100 --> 00:04:40.667
I could -- I didn't even
really see him.

00:04:40.667 --> 00:04:43.000
I just felt him, you know,
And I was like,

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:46.167
Oh, that poor man.
And he was like, "Hi."

00:04:46.167 --> 00:04:50.467
Like, he'd gain like 35 pounds.
And his head was all scratched.

00:04:50.467 --> 00:04:53.934
And I'm like,
"Oh, my God, how are you?

00:04:53.934 --> 00:04:55.734
"Oh, it's so good to see you."

00:04:55.734 --> 00:04:58.200
You know,
and he's so wonderful

00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:00.534
at playing
all those things at once.

00:05:00.534 --> 00:05:05.000
And so, yeah, Ari got in touch
with my agent, said,

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:06.834
"Ari Aster wants
to zoom with you."

00:05:06.834 --> 00:05:08.434
And I got on Zoom and...

00:05:08.434 --> 00:05:10.734
-What did he did?
Did you find out? Like what?

00:05:10.734 --> 00:05:12.901
Was there a piece of your work
that Ari had been drawn to?

00:05:12.901 --> 00:05:15.067
-You know, what he really
liked was "Best in Show."

00:05:15.067 --> 00:05:16.234
-Fantastic.
-Yeah.

00:05:16.234 --> 00:05:18.767
-I mean, a great choice.
-Yeah. Yeah.

00:05:18.767 --> 00:05:21.067
-It's also really fun
because, you know, if you --

00:05:21.067 --> 00:05:23.501
If you see a film
like "Hereditary,"

00:05:23.501 --> 00:05:26.334
you don't think the director
has a great sense of humor.

00:05:26.334 --> 00:05:28.367
And so it's really nice.

00:05:28.367 --> 00:05:29.601
-And he does.
-Yeah.

00:05:29.601 --> 00:05:32.133
-Ari has a great --
He's really smart.

00:05:32.133 --> 00:05:35.534
And he's a cinephile.
You know, he's a filmmaker.

00:05:35.534 --> 00:05:38.634
Like, he's --What I love
about this movie

00:05:38.634 --> 00:05:42.000
is that people you will learn
how to talk about movies

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:43.567
by watching this movie

00:05:43.567 --> 00:05:45.934
because people don't really know
how to talk about it.

00:05:45.934 --> 00:05:47.634
And it's kind of divided.

00:05:47.634 --> 00:05:49.334
But I just think
it's a wonderful.

00:05:49.334 --> 00:05:54.701
Patti LuPone is the mother
who -- owns his whole life.

00:05:54.701 --> 00:05:57.934
But this like, he makes
this landscape, you know,

00:05:57.934 --> 00:06:01.834
and he, Joaquin,
you can't watch him

00:06:01.834 --> 00:06:05.767
and not just have all
these feelings.

00:06:05.767 --> 00:06:07.701
So we all came in.
I came in at the tail end.

00:06:07.701 --> 00:06:09.334
And so did Patty.
-You got -- What are the --

00:06:09.334 --> 00:06:10.934
-We worked two days.

00:06:10.934 --> 00:06:13.734
And, you know, you just hope
for that kind of magic

00:06:13.734 --> 00:06:16.634
when you're working on a film
that just like everything

00:06:16.634 --> 00:06:18.434
that I seem to be doing
at the time

00:06:18.434 --> 00:06:22.801
was had created an atmosphere
around, like, I checked --

00:06:22.801 --> 00:06:25.868
I flew to Atlanta,
and I didn't have my bag,

00:06:25.868 --> 00:06:27.701
and like I was in the wrong,

00:06:27.701 --> 00:06:31.434
I was in the international thing
and I was just like, "Aah!"

00:06:31.434 --> 00:06:33.267
You know I felt like
"Beau is Afraid."

00:06:33.267 --> 00:06:34.434
Was this your fault or did...
Parker was afraid.

00:06:34.434 --> 00:06:36.968
-[ Chuckles ]
-No, it was not my fault.

00:06:36.968 --> 00:06:38.300
-Okay.
-But it should stay...

00:06:38.300 --> 00:06:40.133
-Wrong terminal wasn't your
fault, you don't think?

00:06:40.133 --> 00:06:41.367
[Audience laughs]

00:06:41.367 --> 00:06:42.968
- Maybe. [Chuckles]
You know, it was, like --

00:06:42.968 --> 00:06:44.868
Because, you know,
I worked all night.

00:06:44.868 --> 00:06:46.334
I worked two nights,

00:06:46.334 --> 00:06:50.100
and then I had to catch
the plane after work.

00:06:50.100 --> 00:06:51.200
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:06:51.200 --> 00:06:52.434
-That gets tricky.
-Yeah, that's hard.

00:06:52.434 --> 00:06:53.667
-One of the things they --
-[Chuckles]

00:06:53.667 --> 00:06:55.100
-One of the things
they put you through as well

00:06:55.100 --> 00:06:57.501
is you had to get a full-body
cast made for your --

00:06:57.501 --> 00:06:58.634
-That's right.

00:06:58.634 --> 00:07:00.334
-For your two days
of work on this film.

00:07:00.334 --> 00:07:02.834
A full-body cast,
I've had a head cast made once

00:07:02.834 --> 00:07:04.267
and it's very claustrophobic.

00:07:04.267 --> 00:07:06.367
How did you take to having
a full-body cast?

00:07:06.367 --> 00:07:08.567
-Yeah, so the --
But it's that material

00:07:08.567 --> 00:07:10.267
that when you go to a dentist

00:07:10.267 --> 00:07:12.601
and they put that mold
in your mouth

00:07:12.601 --> 00:07:13.934
and then, like,
[Click].

00:07:13.934 --> 00:07:15.801
I just had that done.

00:07:15.801 --> 00:07:19.467
They covered my entire body
in this for,

00:07:19.467 --> 00:07:20.901
I think a few hours.

00:07:20.901 --> 00:07:22.667
-And are you good
at like sort of...

00:07:22.667 --> 00:07:23.834
-...disassociating?
-Yeah.

00:07:23.834 --> 00:07:25.701
-Absolutely.
That's why I can do what I do.

00:07:25.701 --> 00:07:27.734
-[Chuckles]
-Are you kidding?

00:07:27.734 --> 00:07:30.100
Denial? Yeah.
-[Laughs]

00:07:30.100 --> 00:07:32.067
-Compartmentalizing?
You bet.

00:07:32.067 --> 00:07:33.534
Yeah. Fantastic.

00:07:33.534 --> 00:07:35.501
-Only because,
just commitment to the role

00:07:35.501 --> 00:07:37.200
and because you already
mentioned it.

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:38.501
Because I also much like Ari,

00:07:38.501 --> 00:07:41.367
a huge fan of your
performance in "Best in Show,"

00:07:41.367 --> 00:07:43.200
and the braces,
you actually --

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:44.901
Is it true that
you got the braces?

00:07:44.901 --> 00:07:47.367
-Yes.
-Beforehand?

00:07:47.367 --> 00:07:50.901
-Well, okay, so you know
these movies are...

00:07:50.901 --> 00:07:54.100
Chris, allows us to do --
They're all improvised.

00:07:54.100 --> 00:07:55.367
They're unscripted.

00:07:55.367 --> 00:07:56.834
And we were just sitting
at lunch once

00:07:56.834 --> 00:07:59.968
and he's like, "What if you
and..." -- What if you two?

00:07:59.968 --> 00:08:02.901
I was with Hitchcock,
who plays my husband.

00:08:02.901 --> 00:08:04.734
"What if you two had braces?"

00:08:04.734 --> 00:08:08.234
I went, "Oh, we're there.
We're there."

00:08:08.234 --> 00:08:11.534
And so Hitchcock
got a retainer

00:08:11.534 --> 00:08:14.267
that he could just put
in his mouth and take out,

00:08:14.267 --> 00:08:16.067
and it made him talk
with a lisp.

00:08:16.067 --> 00:08:18.567
But I didn't want to be,
you know,

00:08:18.567 --> 00:08:20.534
his wife talking
with the lisp either.

00:08:20.534 --> 00:08:22.400
And I thought
it would be fun to --

00:08:22.400 --> 00:08:23.501
to get braces.

00:08:23.501 --> 00:08:25.701
I'd had them when I was 12.
-Yeah.

00:08:25.701 --> 00:08:27.667
Not a lot of people
do a second round.

00:08:27.667 --> 00:08:29.200
-No, that's right.

00:08:29.200 --> 00:08:32.667
But people were getting braces,
as a lot of people.

00:08:32.667 --> 00:08:34.868
-Yeah, that's true.
That was the moment.

00:08:34.868 --> 00:08:36.067
-And I actually
kind of needed them

00:08:36.067 --> 00:08:37.133
because this tooth
was a little...

00:08:37.133 --> 00:08:38.300
-So they
were actually like...

00:08:38.300 --> 00:08:40.067
Not that I would have run out
and be like,

00:08:40.067 --> 00:08:41.400
Oh, I'm going to get braces.

00:08:41.400 --> 00:08:42.567
-So it's actually a nice cover

00:08:42.567 --> 00:08:43.968
that you got to do it
in a movie.

00:08:43.968 --> 00:08:45.167
-Yeah.
-And then everybody afterward...

00:08:45.167 --> 00:08:46.834
-It all works out.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:08:46.834 --> 00:08:48.601
-And then you were like,
"You're great in that movie.

00:08:48.601 --> 00:08:50.501
And also your teeth
look fantastic."

00:08:50.501 --> 00:08:52.901
-Yes.
Thank you.

00:08:52.901 --> 00:08:54.467
-Congrats on the film and thanks
so much for being here.

00:08:54.467 --> 00:08:56.501
-Oh, it was great being here.
-You're such a delight.

00:08:56.501 --> 00:08:58.033
- Guys. That's Parker Posey.

00:08:58.033 --> 00:08:59.868
"Beau is Afraid" is in
theaters now.

00:08:59.868 --> 00:09:01.634
We'll be right back with more
"Late Night."

